{
    "background_story": "Ayaan was a young artist who lived in a small village nestled in the rolling hills of rural Japan. He was known for his exquisite paper lanterns, which he crafted with precision and love. Every summer, the village would hold a festival to celebrate the full moon, and Ayaan's lanterns were always the centerpiece. He would spend hours designing and building the intricate lanterns, carefully selecting the colors and patterns to match the mood of the season. As the sun set on the festival day, Ayaan would light his lanterns, and the village would be bathed in a warm, ethereal glow. The villagers believed that Ayaan's lanterns brought good fortune and prosperity, and they would often make wishes as they gazed at the soft light. Ayaan's lanterns became a symbol of hope and joy, and his craft was passed down through generations as a reminder of the beauty of tradition and community.",
